  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional arrival directions</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"Turn left on Main Street. Don’t park in front of the building. Call me when you arrive."</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Where is the subject in these sentences?</li>
      <li>What form does each verb have? Look for endings.</li>
      <li>How do we make an instruction negative?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. The route and contact reasons make each positive or negative action necessary.">
    <h3>Discovery check: choose the pattern</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Every instruction starts with the verb. Read the reason after the gap, then choose the positive or negative chunk.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Turn">Turn</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Don’t turn">Don’t turn</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Call">Call</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Don’t call">Don’t call</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The route requires a right at the supermarket: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Turn" data-hint="The route requires the action, so use the positive base-verb chunk Turn." data-fix="Turn right at the supermarket, and the office is on your left." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> right at the supermarket, and the office is on your left.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Oak Street is closed for repairs: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Don’t turn" data-hint="The closed street makes the left turn an action to avoid." data-fix="Don’t turn left on Oak Street; it’s closed for repairs." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> left on Oak Street.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The fictional host must open a locked gate after the visitor arrives: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Call" data-hint="The host needs the arrival signal, so use the positive instruction Call." data-fix="Call me when you arrive, and I’ll open the gate." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> me when you arrive.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The fictional host’s quiet hours begin at ten: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Don’t call" data-hint="The time restriction makes calling after ten an action to avoid." data-fix="Don’t call me after ten tonight; I go to bed early." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> me after ten tonight.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The group needs a reservation by the window: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Call" data-hint="Getting the reservation requires the positive action Call." data-fix="Call the restaurant and ask for a table by the window." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the restaurant and ask for a table by the window.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The garage sign says residents only: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Don’t turn" data-hint="The access restriction makes entering the garage an action to avoid." data-fix="Don’t turn into the parking garage; it’s for residents only." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> into the parking garage.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: the base verb does everything</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>An ordinary imperative starts with the <strong>base verb</strong> and usually has no written subject. <span class="ex">Don’t</span> makes it negative, <span class="ex">please</span> can soften it, and <span class="ex">let’s</span> turns it into a shared plan.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>purpose</th><th>pattern</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>positive instruction</b></td><td>base verb, no subject</td><td>Turn left at the light.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>negative</b></td><td>don’t + base verb</td><td>Don’t park here.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>softened</b></td><td>please at the start or the end</td><td>Please wait here. Wait here, please.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>suggestion with us</b></td><td>let’s + base verb</td><td>Let’s take a break.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">The verb never changes. No <span class="ex">-s</span>, no <span class="ex">-ing</span>, and no <span class="ex">to</span>: say <span class="ex">Don’t worry</span>, not <span class="ex">Don’t to worry</span>.</p>

  <h2>Get to the root: an ordinary imperative addresses you</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>An ordinary imperative begins with the base verb because <span class="ex">you</span>, the listener or listeners, is understood from the situation: <span class="ex">(You) open the file</span>. English normally leaves <span class="ex">you</span> unspoken because the addressee is already obvious. This is why there is no third-person <span class="ex">-s</span>: <span class="ex">she opens</span> is a statement about her, but <span class="ex">Open the file</span> directly addresses the listener.</p>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Why context is essential</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Turn left.</span> can be helpful directions. <span class="ex">Take one tablet with food.</span> can be medical instructions. <span class="ex">Don’t move.</span> can be a warning. The grammar is the same; purpose, relationship, and tone determine how the listener understands it.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>The base verb makes imperatives direct and efficient</h3>
  <p>There is no tense ending, third-person <span class="ex">-s</span>, or auxiliary in a positive imperative: <span class="ex">Wait. Be careful. Have a seat.</span> For the negative, <span class="ex">don’t</span> carries the negative grammar, even with <span class="ex">be</span>: <span class="ex">Don’t be late.</span></p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>meaning</th><th>form</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>do the action</td><td>base verb</td><td>Save the document.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>do not do the action</td><td>don’t + base verb</td><td>Don’t delete the original.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>emphasize</td><td>do + base verb</td><td>Do come in. Do be careful.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Don’t</span> is the normal conversational negative. Signs, formal rules, and especially firm warnings may use full <span class="ex">Do not</span>: <span class="ex">Do not enter</span>. Positive <span class="ex">do</span> adds warmth or insistence rather than ordinary form: <span class="ex">Do come in</span> can sound warmly encouraging; <span class="ex">Do be careful</span> strongly emphasizes the warning.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>When you is spoken</h3>
    <p>An explicit <span class="ex">you</span> is possible when the speaker contrasts listeners or adds emphasis: <span class="ex">You wait here; Marco, come with me.</span> It can sound strong, so it is not the neutral default. Words that name the addressee sit outside the core instruction: <span class="ex">Maya, open the file</span> and <span class="ex">Everyone, take a seat</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each workplace, test, reception, reminder, or support context points to one natural imperative chunk.">
    <h3>Drill 1: choose the natural chunk</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Read the whole line first. Then choose the chunk that fits the situation.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Take">Take</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Ask">Ask</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Don’t worry">Don’t worry</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Don’t forget">Don’t forget</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The building guide says the office is on the fifth floor: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Take" data-hint="The route requires using the elevator, and Take fits the travel chunk take the elevator." data-fix="Take the elevator to the fifth floor." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the elevator to the fifth floor.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The fictional learner is prepared but anxious: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Don’t worry" data-hint="The supportive message tells the learner to stop worrying, not to remember or ask something." data-fix="Don’t worry about the test; you’re ready." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> about the test; you’re ready.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The visitor needs a badge from the receptionist: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Ask" data-hint="The visitor must request the badge from a person, so use Ask." data-fix="Ask the receptionist for a visitor badge." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the receptionist for a visitor badge.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The password is required every morning: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Don’t forget" data-hint="The reminder tells the listener to remember a necessary item." data-fix="Don’t forget your password; you’ll need it every morning." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> your password.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The clinic receptionist directs a waiting patient to a chair: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Take" data-hint="The conventional welcoming instruction is Take a seat." data-fix="Take a seat; the doctor will call you soon." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a seat; the doctor will call you soon.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The trainer invites clarification questions: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Ask" data-hint="If something is unclear, the listener should ask the trainer." data-fix="Ask me if anything is unclear." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> me if anything is unclear.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>Closing the file without saving would lose the work: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Don’t forget" data-hint="The risk makes this a reminder to remember the saving step." data-fix="Don’t forget to save the file before you close it." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> to save the file before you close it.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>The trainer normalizes first attempts: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Don’t worry" data-hint="The supportive message tells the learner not to be anxious about a possible mistake." data-fix="Don’t worry if you make a mistake; everyone does at first." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> if you make a mistake.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Grammar is simple; social meaning is not</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A bare imperative can sound neutral, friendly, urgent, or rude. The relationship and situation decide. Instructions on a screen (<span class="ex">Enter your password</span>) are neutral. The same direct form spoken to a coworker may need softening.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>form</th><th>typical effect</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>bare imperative</td><td>clear, direct</td><td>Send me the address.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>please + imperative</td><td>polite but still direct</td><td>Please send me the address.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Can you…?</td><td>frames it as a request</td><td>Can you send me the address?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Let’s + base verb</td><td>includes speaker and listener</td><td>Let’s check the address.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>When imperatives are friendly</h3>
    <p>Imperatives are normal and can sound warm for directions, instructions, offers, and encouragement: <span class="ex">Take a seat. Have some coffee. Don’t worry.</span> A clear helpful context and a warm voice make these social actions different from an order.</p>
    <p>For a request to a stranger or manager, soften it: add <span class="ex">please</span>, or turn it into a question with <span class="ex">Can you…?</span> from the <a href="/lessons/a1/can-for-ability-and-permission/">can lesson</a>. <span class="ex">Can you send me the file, please?</span></p>
  </div>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Please</span> can go at the beginning or end, but it does not automatically make every command appropriate. Relationship, choice, urgency, voice, and facial expression still matter. When the listener can reasonably refuse, <span class="ex">Can you…?</span> often gives a request more room than an imperative.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: intonation tells the listener how to receive the imperative</h3>
    <p>The grammar may stay identical while the voice changes the social message. Keep the action and the key information clear; use a calm, warm voice for help and a stronger fall for an urgent warning.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>purpose</th><th>spoken model</th><th>voice</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>helpful direction</td><td><b>TURN</b> left at the <b>BANK</b>.</td><td>clear and calm</td></tr>
        <tr><td>polite request</td><td>Please send the <b>FILE</b>.</td><td>warm, not clipped</td></tr>
        <tr><td>urgent warning</td><td><b>DON’T TOUCH</b> that!</td><td>strong stress and a firm fall</td></tr>
        <tr><td>shared suggestion</td><td>Let’s <b>START</b>.</td><td>inclusive and encouraging</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>A bare imperative with a flat, hard voice can sound like an order. A calm rhythm, useful context, and an appropriate <span class="ex">please</span> or <span class="ex">Can you…?</span> often matter more than adding extra words.</p>
    <p>In neutral U.S. speech, <span class="ex">don’t</span> is one compact beat /doʊnt/. Before a consonant, its final <span class="ex">t</span> may be light or unreleased, as in <span class="ex">Don’t move</span>, but the negative meaning must remain clear. <span class="ex">Let’s</span> sounds /lets/; keep the final <span class="ex">s</span> audible so the complete chunk remains clear. In writing, the apostrophe and sentence structure distinguish <span class="ex">let’s</span> from the ordinary verb <span class="ex">lets</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read imperative purpose, politeness, and intonation check">
    <h2>Listen: what social action did the speaker perform?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each fictional line once with natural U.S. rhythm and once more slowly. Use the situation, form, and voice to identify the social action. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished a neutral instruction, friendly offer, polite request, urgent warning, shared suggestion, and negative shared suggestion.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Imperative listening purposes">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="neutral instruction">neutral instruction</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="friendly offer">friendly offer</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="polite request">polite request</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="urgent warning">urgent warning</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="shared suggestion">shared suggestion</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="negative shared suggestion">negative shared suggestion</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Clip 1 gives a screen user a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="neutral instruction" data-hint="A standard interface step directly tells the user which button to select." data-fix="Select Continue: neutral instruction." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Clip 2 gives a guest a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="friendly offer" data-hint="Have some coffee presents something enjoyable to the guest in a warm voice." data-fix="Have some coffee: friendly offer." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Clip 3 makes a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="polite request" data-hint="Can you and please frame the action as a request that the coworker can answer." data-fix="Can you send me the file, please: polite request." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Clip 4 delivers an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="urgent warning" data-hint="Strong stress and immediate danger make Don’t touch that a warning." data-fix="Don’t touch that: urgent warning." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Clip 5 makes a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="shared suggestion" data-hint="Let’s includes the speaker and listener in the proposed break." data-fix="Let’s take a break: shared suggestion." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Clip 6 makes a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="negative shared suggestion" data-hint="Let’s not proposes that the group avoid one plan together." data-fix="Let’s not drive tonight: negative shared suggestion." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
      <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>
    <details class="thinking-card">
      <summary>Open the teacher script after checking</summary>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="ex">Select Continue.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Have some coffee.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Can you send me the file, please?</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Don’t touch that!</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Let’s take a break.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">Let’s not drive tonight.</span></li>
      </ol>
      <p>Use calm, level delivery for the screen instruction, warmth for the offer and suggestion, room for an answer in the request, and a firm falling warning only for the immediate danger.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <h3>Let’s: the imperative for shared plans</h3>
  <p><span class="ex">Let’s + base verb</span> suggests a plan that includes the speaker and the listener together: <span class="ex">Let’s take a break. Let’s meet at the café at noon.</span> The listener can agree or suggest something else, so it feels like an invitation, not a command.</p>
  <p class="note-line">The apostrophe matters: <span class="ex">let’s</span> means <span class="ex">let us</span> in a suggestion, while <span class="ex">lets</span> is an ordinary present-simple verb meaning “allows”: <span class="ex">The app lets us change the language.</span></p>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Useful special forms</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Let me…</span> often offers or announces the speaker’s action: <span class="ex">Let me help you</span> and <span class="ex">Let me check</span>. In some situations it asks permission: <span class="ex">Let me try</span>. <span class="ex">Let’s not…</span> makes a negative shared suggestion: <span class="ex">Let’s not be late.</span> Adding <span class="ex">you</span> is unusual and often emphatic or contrastive: <span class="ex">You wait here; Marco, come with me.</span></p>
  </div>

  <h3>Build clear procedures</h3>
  <p>Good instructions divide a process into manageable actions and show the order. Use sequencing words such as <span class="ex">first, next, then, after that, finally</span>: <span class="ex">First, open the app. Then select “Settings.” Finally, save your changes.</span> Keep one main action in each step when clarity matters.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-answer-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used the base verb for positive instructions and Don’t plus the base verb for negative instructions, including be.">
    <h3>Drill 2: type the first word of the instruction</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Type the base verb with no ending and no <b>to</b>, or type <b>Don’t</b> for a negative. Use the cue in parentheses.</p>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The practice form needs the sample code at the top: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Write" data-hint="A positive instruction begins directly with the base verb Write." data-fix="Write the sample code at the top of the form." aria-label="First word of a positive instruction to put a sample code on a form" /> the sample code at the top of the form. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(to write)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The visitor must bring the badge tomorrow: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Don’t" data-hint="The reminder tells the visitor not to forget, so begin with Don’t." data-fix="Don’t forget your badge tomorrow." aria-label="First word of a negative reminder about a visitor badge" /> forget your badge tomorrow. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(make it negative)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The route card identifies the first exit after the bridge: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Take" data-hint="A positive direction begins directly with the base verb Take." data-fix="Take the first exit after the bridge." aria-label="First word of a positive route instruction about an exit" /> the first exit after the bridge. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(to take)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The fictional meeting starts exactly at nine: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Don’t" data-hint="A negative instruction with be uses Don’t be, not Don’t is." data-fix="Don’t be late; the meeting starts at nine." aria-label="First word of a negative instruction with be before a meeting" /> be late; the meeting starts at nine. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(negative with be)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The fictional customer is ready to pay: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Ask" data-hint="A positive instruction begins directly with the base verb Ask." data-fix="Ask the server for the check." aria-label="First word of a positive restaurant instruction to request the check" /> the server for the check. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(to ask)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The posted safety sign keeps the entrance clear: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Don’t" data-hint="The sign prohibits parking, so begin with the negative Don’t." data-fix="Don’t park in front of the entrance." aria-label="First word of a negative parking instruction at an entrance" /> park in front of the entrance. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(make it negative)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
