  <h2>Notice and repair errors</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="you" data-hint="The requested target is a neutral direction with no contrast between listeners, so the understood subject stays unspoken." data-fix="Turn left at the bank." data-why="A neutral ordinary imperative normally leaves the understood listener you unspoken. Explicit you remains possible for contrast or emphasis.">
    <h3>Drill 6: drop the extra subject</h3>
    <p>Rewrite “You turn left at the bank” as the neutral, unmarked direction with no contrast between listeners. Tap the one extra word.</p>
    <div class="spot-options">
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="you">You</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="turn">turn</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="left">left</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="bank">at the bank.</button>
    </div>
    <p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="to" data-hint="Don’t is followed directly by the base verb worry, without infinitive to." data-fix="Don’t worry about the report." data-why="After don’t, use the base verb directly, with no to.">
    <h3>Drill 7: keep the verb bare after don’t</h3>
    <p>The supportive message should say “Don’t worry about the report.” Tap the extra word in “Don’t to worry about the report.”</p>
    <div class="spot-options">
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="don’t">Don’t</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="to">to</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="worry">worry</button>
